  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Questions in Swahili keep normal word order: the question word simply sits where the answer would go. Je at the start turns a sentence into a yes-or-no question.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Unataka nini?", "base": "What do you want?" },
      { "target": "Una miaka mingapi?", "base": "How old are you?" },
      { "target": "Kwa nini?", "base": "Why?" },
      { "target": "Kwa sababu…", "base": "Because…" },
      { "target": "Unasemaje kwa Kiswahili?", "base": "How do you say it in Swahili?" },
      { "target": "Ina maana gani?", "base": "What does it mean?" }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "question-words",
        "title": "Question words stay put",
        "body": [
          "Swahili doesn’t move the question word to the front. It goes where the answer goes: **Unataka nini?** (you want what?) → **Nataka chai**. **Unaenda wapi?** → **Ninaenda sokoni**.",
          "For a yes-or-no question, just raise your voice at the end, or start with **Je**: **Je, una watoto?** (do you have children?)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "kwa-nini",
        "title": "Why and because",
        "body": [
          "**Kwa nini** is literally “for what”. Answer with **kwa sababu** (“for the reason”): **Kwa nini hutaki chai? Kwa sababu sipendi chai.**",
          "“How many” agrees with its noun: **shilingi ngapi**, **watoto wangapi**, **miaka mingapi**."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Asking someone’s age",
        "body": [
          "Asking **Una miaka mingapi?** is ordinary between young people. With elders, it’s better to ask about their children and grandchildren than about their age."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
